course: dictionary
lesson: 13164
title: Fortification: walls and added vitamins
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A fortification is something built to make a place stronger against attack. The clue is in the word: fortification makes you think of a fort, a strong place. «The knights built stone fortifications around the castle to keep out the enemy.» The words built, keep out and enemy tell you that fortifications are walls or towers that protect the castle.
explain: Test your meaning. Swap it in: «The knights built walls and towers around the castle to keep out the enemy.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Fortification can also mean the act of making a place stronger. «The soldiers spent the winter on the fortification of the harbour.» Here it is the work of making the harbour safer, not a wall itself.
explain: In food, fortification means adding vitamins or minerals to food. «The cereal box says it is fortified with iron» tells you that iron was added to make the cereal healthier. The same idea, making something stronger, is used in a different way.
fact: A fortification is a wall, tower, ditch or other structure built to defend a place from attack.
fact: Castles, city walls, hill forts and Hadrian's Wall are all examples of fortifications.
fact: Coastal fortifications defended harbours from attack from the sea.
fact: Food fortification means adding vitamins or minerals to food, such as vitamins added to cereal.
fact: «Fortified» cereals and flour have extra vitamins, iron or folic acid added to them.
exercise: «The town built new stone fortifications after the attack.» What are fortifications here? | Roads for cars | Shops and houses for the people | *Walls and towers built to defend the town | Gardens and parks for play
exercise: Which of these is an example of food fortification? | A bread roll with nothing added | Plain porridge made only from oats | *Breakfast cereal with vitamins added | A cup of plain water
exercise: «Coastal fortifications defended the harbour.» Where were these fortifications? | Inside a school hall | *At the edge of the sea | Deep under a river | In the middle of a desert
test: Which of these is a fortification? | A library book | *A hill fort | A kite | A breakfast bowl
test: What does food fortification mean? | Cooking food over a fire | *Adding vitamins or minerals to food | Making food with no salt at all | Throwing food away after a meal
exam: Which phrase means walls built along the sea shore to defend a harbour? | Food fortification | *Coastal fortifications | Garden walls | Stone fortifications
